module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**unary minus absorbs a following additive operator** (invalid state: the seed parser reads `-1 - j` as `-(1 - j)` and `-a + b` as `-(a + b)`. v1.compiler.parse parse_prefix parses the operand of ShMinus at min_bp 12, while extdeps.languages.dag.syntax gives infix `+` and `-` left_bp 13 (and `*`, `/` 15), so the operand loop keeps consuming every additive and multiplicative operator after the prefix. Harm: silent wrongness. The program is accepted and computes a different integer, with no diagnostic on any route that reads the seed's parse tree, which is the interpreter and every emitter.)",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: a negative literal standing alone (`last_rank: -1`, `{ -1 }`) is unaffected, as is any unary minus whose operand is followed by a comparison, a logical operator or a closing bracket. Only an additive or multiplicative infix operator that follows the operand is absorbed. A multiplicative absorption (`-a * b` read as `-(a * b)`) agrees in value except at the overflow boundary, so the additive case is the one that changes answers.",

    "RECEIPT 2026-10-19 (std.integer Int delivery 1): the signed grounding fold first spelled the negative encoding `-1 - j`, and `Negative { pred: 2 }` emitted `1` instead of `-3`. The emitted-bytes witness test.claim.integer_signed_grounding_emit_witness caught it, and a probe module confirmed the interpreter agrees with the emitter: `-1 - 2 == 0 - 3` is false. The fold now spells `0 - 1 - k`. A corpus census at that head, over every .dag file outside the stage0 mirrors, found no other expression of the exposed shape (a unary minus in expression-start position whose operand is followed by an arithmetic infix operator), validated against the known positives above.",

    "Rung found at: silent wrongness, which is outside the ladder and forbidden. It is latent today only because the census found no exposed site, and nothing stops the next author writing one. Ceiling: structurally guaranteed. Prefix binding power is a modeled grammar fact, and a prefix operand bound above every infix operator it should not absorb cannot misparse. Next trigger, at capability grain: prefix operators' binding powers declared in the language's syntax spec beside OperatorSpec (not the literal 12 in parse_prefix), read by the seed parser and by v2's grammar-row parse alike. A discriminating fixture must show `-1 - 2` evaluating to -3 on the interpreter and in the emitted Rust, and the v2 native parse must be checked for the same reading before either is called fixed.",

    "CLIMB (dashboard node adhoc-9102e2d6-92f): the trigger above fired. Prefix operand binding power is now DECLARED beside OperatorSpec -- std.syntax PrefixOperatorSpec, SyntaxSpec.prefix_operators, instances extdeps.languages.dag.syntax dag_prefix_operators and extdeps.languages.rust.syntax rust_prefix_operators, both 17 -- and v1.compiler.parse parse_prefix reads it through find_prefix_operand_bp for BOTH `!` and `-`; no literal 12 survives, and an undeclared prefix spelling refuses with a located parse error rather than inventing a power. 17 is above `*` `/` `%` (15) and every comparison and logical row, and equal to `|>`, below `.`: the operand still absorbs a pipe and a member chain, so the authored `!xs |> any(p)` shape (census below) keeps its reading. EVIDENCE BY EXECUTION on the interpreter, test.claim.unary_prefix_binding_witness, nine arms run through `gunbc run --function` on a seed built from the pre-fix parse mirror and again after the mirror regeneration. OLD SEED: `-1 - 2 == 0 - 3` returned false, `let r = -a + b; r == 1` false, `-a - b - c == 0 - 9` false; the controls `-(1 - 2) == 1`, `-a * b == 0 - 6` (agrees in value, as the row says), `-1 < 0`, `-3 == 0 - 3`, `!false && !false` and the `!xs |> any` pipe arm returned true. FIXED SEED: all nine true. The regenerated mirrors (std_syntax, extdeps_languages_dag_syntax, extdeps_languages_rust_syntax, v1_compiler_parse) are a fixed point: a second claim_executor --regen-round-cost round converged in zero stages and left the tree digest unchanged. CENSUS at main 0ed9cbec4a3 (token scan over every .dag outside the stage0 mirrors for a prefix `-` or `!` in prefix position whose operand is followed by `+ - * / %`): zero authored sites; the only prefix-then-infix shape is `!x |> any(...)` (eight sites), whose reading is unchanged because the declared power absorbs `|>`.",

    "RUNG AFTER THE CLIMB, by path. v1 interpreter and every emitter that reads the seed's parse tree: mechanically preventable (rung 2): the wrong state is still writable by lowering an operand_bp in the table, and what blocks it is the witness above staying enrolled. v2 native parse: v2.extdeps.languages.dag does NOT read std.syntax. It carries its own infix table (dag_infix_operators, powers 1..7, with `|>` the LOWEST row) and expresses prefix reach structurally, `unary_expr` = prefix-operator then `unary_expr`, so a prefix operand can never absorb an infix row; its reading of `-1 - 2` and `-a * b` is the correct one by construction. That is also why v2 reads `!xs |> any(p)` as `(!xs) |> any(p)`, a standing v1/v2 divergence on pipe precedence that this change neither introduces nor removes. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER (capability): a derived check that every declared prefix operand_bp exceeds the left_bp of every arithmetic row of the same SyntaxSpec (which makes the invalid table unwritable as an accepted environment), and the v1/v2 operator tables read from one authority.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.languages.dag.syntax", decl_name: "dag_prefix_operators", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.unary_prefix_binding_witness", decl_name: "minus_one_minus_two_is_minus_three_test", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
